Alright, so, when it comes to the current time, R provides different object classes for handling time points.

00:00:17.464 --> 00:00:22.464
So we have this POSIXct, which is used if time zones and daylight saving is important.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:30.524
And POSIXlt, this is as before, but also it stores elements as list components.

00:00:32.154 --> 00:00:35.154
And then here you can see that if I ask R

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.470
to give me an output of sys.time, then what do I get? I get year, month, day,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.550
Hour, minute, second and the time zone. And indeed, this is today's

00:00:55.523 --> 00:00:57.523
date, and then the time, and the time zone, and then if I now ask R to give me his date, then I get the current date.

00:00:59.803 --> 00:01:01.803
And CET stands for the Central European Time.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.620
And to UTC for Coordinated Universal Time. So, standard byte formats cannot span very long periods while maintaining precision.

00:01:16.138 --> 00:01:18.138
