00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:23.328
from a reference time point, epoch, often this 1st January 1970.

00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:37.832
So, let us look how the internal storage looks like for this time. And indeed, you can see that as if I try to coerce this time to numeric, then I get this huge number.

00:01:37.944 --> 00:01:42.944
And this is actually the number of seconds since that particular date.

00:01:48.986 --> 00:01:51.986
We could convert it to the time point, using as.POSIXct, so we try to coerce it to that class.

00:01:51.923 --> 00:01:59.923
Where we need to select the origin. So we can see that, indeed, we managed to coerce it to that particular class.

00:02:05.396 --> 00:02:09.396
So, there is this, year two thousand problem, where before, years were stored with two digits.

00:02:13.798 --> 00:02:16.798
And after 1999 certain systems rolled back to 1900.

00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:23.547
And there's also this year 2038 problem. So we've signed 32 bit seconds

00:02:26.085 --> 00:02:28.085
since 1970, values overflow at

00:02:34.949 --> 00:02:39.949
2038, so later times cannot be represented. Here, let us look at day conversions. So, for example, here, we could coerce this Sys.Date

00:02:39.663 --> 00:02:46.663
To POSIXct, using that particular date as an origin, and indeed, we managed to do it.

00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:53.867
We could also use the function as.Date, and then here you can see that, once again, I tried to coerce this Sys.Date,

00:02:58.666 --> 00:03:04.666
and then I get today's date. And then if I try to coerce something from a German date format, where we have

00:03:05.066 --> 00:03:12.066
first day, month, and year, so basically we need to tell the function what is the format, and indeed we get what we wanted. 

00:03:12.429 --> 00:03:19.429
And then here, you have different codes for different values. So, for example, D stands for day of the month,

00:03:25.884 --> 00:03:29.884
but capital B for month, full name, etc. Here, when it comes to time conversions, we could, coerce time to POSIXct

00:03:33.359 --> 00:03:38.359
once again, using this as.POSIXct, and then by selecting the origin.

00:03:45.497 --> 00:03:48.497
And then you could also, convert German format, where the time is first and the date last, so you can see that the time is first, the date is last.

00:03:47.985 --> 00:03:55.985
And then we need to select the origin, and we need to select the format. So indeed, we selected the format, and we got our result.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:10.460
Right, and here, once again, a table with certain codes, which stand for certain values. So here, even more codes and more values, so you can see that, like, these conversion codes are very flexible, and many

00:04:11.029 --> 00:04:21.029
values have been already predefined. Similarly here, for example, we have different codes for hours, days, months, etc, so I definitely suggest that you have a look and play with them.

00:04:30.381 --> 00:04:32.381
And similarly here. We have different codes for weekdays, there's decimal points, so really, that package and that function

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:38.304
are very flexible, and then they can, we can convert many different time and date formats. 

00:04:47.042 --> 00:04:49.042
Here, let us try to extract from a time point. So basically here, let us define this variable t, and this is, once again.

00:05:01.557 --> 00:05:05.557
This, object caused to POSIX CT class. So, here, if we now define this variable t, and then we apply to t function weekdays, and we set a parameter abbreviate to TRUE, then we get Friday.

