If we abbreviate, if we set now,

00:05:18.143 --> 00:05:20.143
this function months to t, and then we select a parameter abbreviate, then we get an April, and this is once again the case.

00:05:20.524 --> 00:05:27.524
And then, if we apply a function quarters to t, then we get the second quarter, and this is the case. 

00:05:28.064 --> 00:05:37.064
So here, now we try to go from as.POSIXct to as.POSIXlt, so here it uses

00:05:42.958 --> 00:05:44.958
list components, so for example, here we could access different elements by seconds, minutes, hours, mday,

00:05:45.412 --> 00:05:50.412
month, year, etc. So, for example, here, if I would like to access the year.

00:05:56.111 --> 00:06:02.111
I get 120, because this is a year since 1900. And then here, if I would like to access the time zone, this is indeed Central European time.

00:06:03.164 --> 00:06:11.164
Alright, and then we could also add time to time points, and we could create time point sequences. So, for example, here,

00:06:11.306 --> 00:06:17.306
once again, I defined that variable T, which is this coerces class

00:06:17.488 --> 00:06:28.488
POSIXct, and then I would like to add 360 seconds, so indeed, I just, typed T plus 3600 seconds.

00:06:27.603 --> 00:06:34.603
And indeed, you can see that I added these seconds to my original value.

00:06:35.131 --> 00:06:41.131
And then, we could, also add days. For example, here, if I define this variable T.

00:06:48.228 --> 00:06:51.228
And then I try to coerce this character value to as.Date, and then I add 30 days, then I get, indeed

00:06:58.414 --> 00:07:00.414
A correct number here. And indeed, the first date is in April, the second is in May.

00:07:00.407 --> 00:07:05.407
And then we could also create sequences, so you can see that, I could create a sequence

00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:16.365
from a particular day in April, to a particular day in April, and then by 3 days, and indeed, you can see that I created a sequence

00:07:21.626 --> 00:07:24.626
using this as date functions. Here we could also create time point differences using different functions.

00:07:28.432 --> 00:07:31.432
So once again, we have these two objects, T1 and T2, and then,

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:43.970
they were created using this as POSIXct. And then you can see that if I now subtract T1 from T2, then I get the time difference of 3.5 hours

00:07:46.707 --> 00:07:51.707
as an output. We could also apply a function difftime, and we get exactly the same output.

00:07:52.102 --> 00:07:58.102
We could also apply function as numeric to difftime, and then we get just a number 3.5,

00:08:05.007 --> 00:08:10.007
and then we could also get these two parameters to function difftime, and then change the units. So here, before we had

00:08:10.233 --> 00:08:16.233
hours, now we would like to have seconds, and indeed, now we have the time difference in seconds.

00:08:24.574 --> 00:08:26.574
We could also transform that output into the numeric value, and indeed, we get a correct output.

